So targeting aging is the most important thing,

266
00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:42.900
even more than obesity, way more than obesity.

267
00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:45.480
So if you can slow down just a little bit,

268
00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:49.530
the aging process, you can do much more than obesity does.

269
00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:53.520
Of course, obesity and smoking are a big second and third,

270
00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:57.960
but they are dwarfed compared to the aging process.

271
00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:02.040
Right, so you began your career looking at yeast

272
00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:06.750
aging in yeast to try to identify what these mechanisms are.

273
00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:10.470
Maybe explain how this is applicable to human health

274
00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:13.350
and what exactly, like how we should think about

275
00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:16.140
what that aging mechanism or process is.

276
00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:17.010
Yes.

277
00:13:17.010 --> 00:13:18.840
So actually I started with Roy Walford

278
00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:20.910
working on humans and mice,

279
00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:23.790
and I understood that it wasn't going anywhere.

280
00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:27.360
So I went back to yeast from humans and mice

281
00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:31.500
which at the time people thought it was a just bad idea,

282
00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:34.710
and so I think that if you look at the genes

283
00:13:34.710 --> 00:13:38.640
that regulate aging in many organisms, and we think humans

284
00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:41.190
because now we've been following people

285
00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:44.130
with a certain mutation, which I'll talk about in a second.

286
00:13:44.130 --> 00:13:46.710
The growth pathways seem to be at the center

287
00:13:46.710 --> 00:13:48.180
of the aging process.

288
00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:52.080
So if you take a yeast, a fly, a mouse,

289
00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:55.950
and now we'll talk about humans, they live a lot longer

290
00:13:55.950 --> 00:14:00.950
if you block IGF-1 insulin and other growth factors.

291
00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:06.360
And not only, they become a lot longer lived

292
00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:10.560
so yeast, for example, by blocking this protein pathway

293
00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:11.460
and then on the other side,

294
00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:13.710
blocking the sugar pathway and then starving them.

295
00:14:13.710 --> 00:14:14.850
They live 10 times longer.

296
00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:18.363
So you completely reprogram their ability, their lifespan.

297
00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:21.960
And this is compressed as you move up.

298
00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:25.980
So in mice, they live about 40 to 50% longer

299
00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:28.650
if they have deficiency in these growth genes,

300
00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:30.390
particularly the growth hormone gene

301
00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:32.550
and the growth hormone receptor gene.

302
00:14:32.550 --> 00:14:36.510
And so when we then study humans,

303
00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:40.170
those deficient in the same gene growth hormone receptor,

304
00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:42.570
we haven't proven yet longevity extension,

305
00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:44.460
but they're protected from cancer.

306
00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:46.530
They're protected from cognitive decline,

307
00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:48.840
they're protected from diabetes.

308
00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:52.590
So they seem to be protected from most of the human

309
00:14:52.590 --> 00:14:55.147
chronic age related disorders.

310
00:14:55.147 --> 00:14:59.490
So we suspect that we are also gonna see lifespan extension.

311
00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:01.437
We're not gonna see 50% like in mice,

312
00:15:01.437 --> 00:15:04.260
but even if we saw 10%, 15%

313
00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:07.620
with very little chronic diseases, that would be,

314
00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:09.210
I think, remarkable.

315
00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:11.670
How do we understand the nature of that compression

316
00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:14.370
as we go from yeast all the way up to humans

317
00:15:14.370 --> 00:15:18.213
and why it doesn't scale in a more linear fashion?

318
00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:22.230
Probably because of evolutionary theories,

319
00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:26.530
meaning that every organism

320
00:15:28.248 --> 00:15:33.248
has evolved by being able to have different modality.

321
00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:35.837
So for example, yeast, you can have a modality

322
00:15:35.837 --> 00:15:37.431
it's a life in the fast lane

323
00:15:37.431 --> 00:15:39.600
and they live about two or three days.

324
00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:42.450
Then you can starve them a little bit

325
00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:46.380
or starve them, and they live about two weeks.

326
00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:48.870
And then you have something called the spore state

327
00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:51.660
and in the spore state, they live 100 times longer

328
00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:54.753
than in the fast lane, so they live years.

329
00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:58.500
So that tells you that probably in every organism,

330
00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:02.910
there's like a reproductive grow mode.

331
00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:06.660
And then there is a maintenance mode

332
00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:08.100
where the decision is made

333
00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:10.650
that there's probably not enough food,

334
00:16:10.650 --> 00:16:12.930
not enough to grow, not enough to reproduce

335
00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:15.337
so I'm just gonna stand by

336
00:16:15.337 --> 00:16:18.420
and try to protect myself as best as I can.

337
00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:19.680
So now of course,

338
00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:22.950
most of us are now reproducing most of the time.

339
00:16:22.950 --> 00:16:25.050
So probably a good idea, right?

340
00:16:25.050 --> 00:16:27.450
To switch to that modality and stay there

341
00:16:27.450 --> 00:16:30.480
until we are ready to reproduce.

342
00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:31.313
Right.

343
00:16:31.313 --> 00:16:33.360
Okay, so then with respect to humans,

344
00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:36.120
you've identified these five pillars of longevity.

345
00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:37.890
So maybe we can walk through them

346
00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:42.890
and then drill down more specifically on the diet piece.

347
00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:45.188
Yes.

348
00:16:45.188 --> 00:16:49.620
So, in this study, you just mentioned,

349
00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:53.730
epidemiology, of course, is very central so lots of studies,

350
00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:56.460
epidemiological studies looking at,

351
00:16:56.460 --> 00:16:58.980
what people that live long eat.

352
00:16:58.980 --> 00:17:01.260
And so one thing we published on a few years ago

353
00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:03.450
was if you look at Americans,

354
00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:05.550
those that have a high protein diet,

355
00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:07.830
they do very poorly compared to those

356
00:17:07.830 --> 00:17:10.110
that have a very low protein diet,

357
00:17:10.110 --> 00:17:13.170
but that's only true up to age 65.

358
00:17:13.170 --> 00:17:16.800
And then after 65, it turns around a little bit,

359
00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:18.780
and those that have a moderate protein intake

360
00:17:18.780 --> 00:17:21.420
do better than those that have a low protein intake.

361
00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:25.534
And I think if you look at the data

362
00:17:25.534 --> 00:17:27.240
and you look at the Harvard studies,

363
00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:28.553
they're supportive of this.

364
00:17:28.553 --> 00:17:32.100
So they will say that a low carbohydrate diet

365
00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:36.480
is bad for you in general, unless it's a plant based

366
00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:37.710
low carbohydrate diet.

367
00:17:37.710 --> 00:17:41.010
They haven't looked so much at the age, dependent effect

368
00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:45.150
but I think we are in agreement that low carbohydrate diet

369
00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:47.610
and high protein are not good for you.

370
00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:48.780
It should be the other way around

371
00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:50.670
low protein and high carbohydrate.

372
00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:53.910
And so epidemiological studies are, I think,

373
00:17:53.910 --> 00:17:56.820
generally consistent with this notion.

374
00:17:56.820 --> 00:18:00.270
So for example, there's another, Lancet meta-analysis,

375
00:18:00.270 --> 00:18:02.817
looking at carbohydrate intake,

376
00:18:02.817 --> 00:18:07.740
and it's better for lifespan to be an 80% carbohydrate diet

377
00:18:07.740 --> 00:18:09.540
than to be in a low carbohydrate diet.

378
00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:13.200
And if you go to very low carbohydrate diet, I say 20%,

379
00:18:13.200 --> 00:18:16.320
there's a 60% increased risk of mortality

380
00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:19.980
compared to the 50, 60% of the calories

381
00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:21.273
coming from carbohydrate.

382
00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:26.370
But then what happens is the people confuse this

383
00:18:26.370 --> 00:18:28.196
with excess carbohydrate.

384
00:18:28.196 --> 00:18:31.890
They think that when I say high carbohydrate diet,

385
00:18:31.890 --> 00:18:34.200
I mean excess carbohydrate.

386
00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:36.840
So we had doctors for example, complain and say,

387
00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:39.720
well, now we are in United States,

388
00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:41.730
70% of people are overweight and obese,

389
00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:44.910
and you're talking about high carbohydrate diet

390
00:18:44.910 --> 00:18:46.770
but in fact, I'm talking about proportions.

391
00:18:46.770 --> 00:18:49.140
So you gotta get food from somewhere,

392
00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:51.360
of course, you have to have the right amount of calories.

393
00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:53.310
You cannot have excess,

394
00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:58.233
but about 50 to 60% should probably come from carbohydrate.

395
00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:00.960
Yeah, so epidemiology shows that

396
00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:03.060
and just to give you an example.

397
00:19:03.060 --> 00:19:04.980
If you think about carbohydrate,

398
00:19:04.980 --> 00:19:09.180
if you look even in a yeast or a mouse,

399
00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:13.230
if you feed 'em a lot of sugar, in most of the cases

400
00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:16.360
you're gonna accelerate some of the aging pathways

401
00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:20.940
on the sugar side, and then even more clearly

402
00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:25.440
on the protein side, but especially on the amino acid

403
00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:27.150
certain amino acid, like methionine

404
00:19:27.150 --> 00:19:29.100
seems to be at the very center

405
00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:33.720
of the acceleration of the aging process.

406
00:19:33.720 --> 00:19:37.800
And this is pillar too.

407
00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:42.800
So basic research and then clinical studies,

408
00:19:43.710 --> 00:19:45.480
I think are pillar three.

409
00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:48.083
They're very supportive of all of this.

410
00:19:48.083 --> 00:19:51.900
And so, for example, in the longevity diet,

411
00:19:51.900 --> 00:19:56.900
we talk about fats, yes but certain type of fats.

412
00:19:57.450 --> 00:19:58.650
And if you look at, for example,

413
00:19:58.650 --> 00:20:01.830
the work by Astoruk in Spain,

414
00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:05.250
thousands of people randomized one group at risk

415
00:20:05.250 --> 00:20:07.590
for cardiovascular disease,

416
00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:10.077
one group placed on a low fat diet

417
00:20:10.077 --> 00:20:15.077
and one group placed on lots of olive oil or lots of nuts.

418
00:20:16.386 --> 00:20:19.140
There was a study that came out like just last week

419
00:20:19.140 --> 00:20:20.790
about that, I think.

420
00:20:20.790 --> 00:20:23.073
There's been many studies followed up.

421
00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:26.430
Maybe there was one last week, I didn't see that one,

422
00:20:26.430 --> 00:20:30.000
but now it's pretty consistent in showing that,

423
00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:35.000
the high olive oil, high nut diet is better for people.

424
00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:36.420
Right which is so interesting

425
00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:37.950
and somewhat counterintuitive

426
00:20:37.950 --> 00:20:41.070
with respect to cardiovascular disease.

427
00:20:41.070 --> 00:20:43.230
Yes, historically and to this day,

428
00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:48.230
lots of prominent experts will say, go to a no fat diet,

429
00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:50.670
no matter what it is.

430
00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:53.880
So sure, it seems to be true.

431
00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:56.850
It's better to not have saturated fats, animal fats

432
00:20:56.850 --> 00:20:59.910
and again, the epidemiology agrees with that

433
00:20:59.910 --> 00:21:04.470
but when we are talking about these monosaturated fats,

434
00:21:04.470 --> 00:21:09.470
olive oil, nuts, probably certain fish like salmon,

435
00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:13.710
that seems to be consistently associated with living longer.

436
00:21:13.710 --> 00:21:16.033
And then, the centenarian studies of course,

437
00:21:16.033 --> 00:21:18.360
one of the most important pillars.

438
00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:22.230
So if a low carbohydrate diet is not so good for you,

439
00:21:22.230 --> 00:21:24.120
let's look around, let's look at Okinawans

440
00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:26.010
let's look at people in Sardinia, in Calabria,

441
00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:30.150
in Loma Linda, in Costa Rica, in Greece,

442
00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:32.850
do they have a low carbohydrate diet or do they have,

443
00:21:32.850 --> 00:21:35.730
as we will expect a high carbohydrate diet in all of them,

444
00:21:35.730 --> 00:21:38.220
a very high carbohydrate diet,

445
00:21:38.220 --> 00:21:39.900
including the Okinawans.

446
00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:42.090
Who used to get about 70% of their calories

447
00:21:42.090 --> 00:21:45.220
from sweet potatoes, as you heard at the conference

448
00:21:46.110 --> 00:21:47.250
from them.

449
00:21:47.250 --> 00:21:50.310
Right, so with all of that,

450
00:21:50.310 --> 00:21:51.990
and of course, it's about

451
00:21:51.990 --> 00:21:53.760
when you're talking about a high carbohydrate diet,

452
00:21:53.760 --> 00:21:57.150
you have to drill down on what that means specifically.

453
00:21:57.150 --> 00:22:01.800
It's not eating pretzels and tons of refined carbohydrates.

454
00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:05.010
These are like whole plant foods for the most part.

455
00:22:05.010 --> 00:22:07.500
Yes. I mean, it can also be whole grains.

456
00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:09.900
So whole grains in this study done by this Norwegian,

457
00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:13.410
this meta-analysis came out a couple of months ago,

458
00:22:13.410 --> 00:22:15.120
by this Norwegian group.

459
00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:18.570
The number one source of food

460
00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:21.930
associated with life expectancy increase was legumes

461
00:22:21.930 --> 00:22:26.580
and number two was whole grain cereals.

462
00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:30.267
So, it's not just, let's say vegetables,

463
00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:41.780
but it's also lots of carbohydrate that may not necessarily

464
00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:47.370
be associated with health.

465
00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:50.220
For example, say whole grain pasta,

466
00:22:50.220 --> 00:22:53.073
but even pasta, it's probably okay.

467
00:22:54.720 --> 00:22:57.120
Certainly it was okay for most of the centenarian

468
00:22:57.120 --> 00:22:58.950
in most areas.

469
00:22:58.950 --> 00:23:02.250
And so, for example, for the Okinawans, the sweet potato.

470
00:23:02.250 --> 00:23:04.680
You wouldn't necessarily think that sweet potato

471
00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:08.520
is that good for you, but it was very good for them

472
00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:13.520
and so I think you cannot for sure have lots of bread

473
00:23:13.770 --> 00:23:17.160
and lots of potatoes and lots of pasta,

474
00:23:17.160 --> 00:23:21.660
but a certain amount of it is probably not bad.

475
00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:23.290
And don't forget that our body

476
00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:29.190
is not fueled by complex carbohydrates

477
00:23:29.190 --> 00:23:31.200
eventually it's fueled by sugar.

478
00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:33.510
So eventually gets turned into pure sugar

479
00:23:33.510 --> 00:23:36.510
and that's what the brain and every other cell wants,

480
00:23:36.510 --> 00:23:40.004
unless you have ketone bodies or fatty acids around.

481
00:23:40.004 --> 00:23:42.510
So yeah, sometimes we forget the basic biochemistry,

482
00:23:42.510 --> 00:23:44.523
but we are fueled by sugar.

483
00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:47.307
So with this understanding,

484
00:23:47.307 --> 00:23:51.180
the kind of basic tenets of what comprises a diet

485
00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:54.150
that promotes longevity based on this research,

486
00:23:54.150 --> 00:23:57.120
where does fasting come in?

487
00:23:57.120 --> 00:24:01.440
What is the relationship between these dietary pillars

488
00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:06.333
and these protocols around fasting or fasting mimicking?

489
00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:10.320
So I think there are two,

490
00:24:10.320 --> 00:24:13.350
I always look first at the safety factor.

491
00:24:13.350 --> 00:24:16.140
So what is it that we don't know if it's safe or not?

492
00:24:16.140 --> 00:24:20.010
And then I just exclude them until we have many decades,

493
00:24:20.010 --> 00:24:22.050
I think of evidence.

494
00:24:22.050 --> 00:24:25.560
But if you look at safety and efficacy, both,

495
00:24:25.560 --> 00:24:28.380
I think you come up with 12 hours every day

496
00:24:28.380 --> 00:24:30.000
as being very solid.

497
00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:32.010
I always say, I've never seen a study

498
00:24:32.010 --> 00:24:34.703
showing that if you do 12 hours of fasting a day,

499
00:24:34.703 --> 00:24:36.480
you are gonna have a problem.

500
00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:38.443
When you get to 16 and as we,

501
00:24:38.443 --> 00:24:40.020
I think already discussed before

502
00:24:40.020 --> 00:24:42.870
you get to 16 hours, you get to breakfast skipping,

503
00:24:42.870 --> 00:24:45.180
I see meta-analysis, not just studies,

504
00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:48.900
but studies of all studies showing increased mortality,

505
00:24:48.900 --> 00:24:49.953
reduced life span.

506
00:24:50.820 --> 00:24:52.590
Now, what is the reason for it?

507
00:24:52.590 --> 00:24:56.310
We don't know, probably there are double edged swords.

508
00:24:56.310 --> 00:24:59.640
So for example, ketone bodies, maybe fatty acids,

509
00:24:59.640 --> 00:25:01.620
ketone bodies are going both ways.

510
00:25:01.620 --> 00:25:04.050
They're helping you on one side and they're hurting you

511
00:25:04.050 --> 00:25:07.380
on the other side, but so 12 hours, very solid.

512
00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:09.690
And they work by Satchin Panda

513
00:25:09.690 --> 00:25:13.260
and everything else is supportive of it.

514
00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:18.260
And then I would say probably people we eat all the time.

515
00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:24.320
And so, thousands of years ago, as humans evolved,

516
00:25:26.010 --> 00:25:30.210
we evolved probably insulin resistance as a way

517
00:25:30.210 --> 00:25:31.863
to survive the winters.

518
00:25:31.863 --> 00:25:35.790
So, you eat as much as you can during the summer,

519
00:25:35.790 --> 00:25:37.380
or whenever food is available,

520
00:25:37.380 --> 00:25:40.560
you become diabetic essentially, prediabetic or diabetic,

521
00:25:40.560 --> 00:25:42.060
you put everything away.

522
00:25:42.060 --> 00:25:44.640
And then the winter comes or some period

523
00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:46.170
where there is no food

524
00:25:46.170 --> 00:25:48.960
and then you become insulin sensitive again.

525
00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:52.050
So I think what happens now

526
00:25:52.050 --> 00:25:55.200
is everybody is insulin resistant all the time

527
